Build Specification — App 32: The Method Tracker
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A tool that lets a student record every method they have learned, with the contexts where it applies, the contexts where it fails, and the examples they have worked. A method is a procedure: a way of solving a problem, analysing data, or making a decision. The Method Tracker records each method with its name, its subject, its applicability conditions, its failure conditions, the worked examples, the student's proficiency level, and the provenance.

It reuses:

the STAG Annotation Editor (App 1)

the Teaching and Training Sandbox (App 16)

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for students, teachers, tutors, and anyone learning a method-heavy subject.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of methods from users.

Store each method with its name, its subject, its description, and its provenance.

Record the applicability conditions: the contexts where the method works.

Record the failure conditions: the contexts where the method does not work.

Record the prerequisites: the concepts or methods required before this one.

Record the worked examples: the problems the student has solved using the method.

Record the student's proficiency level for each method.

Record the date the student learned the method.

Record the date the student last used the method.

Record the relation between methods: which ones are prerequisites, which are alternatives, which are special cases.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by method ID, method name, subject, topic, proficiency level, student, date learned, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the description, the applicability conditions, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by subject, by proficiency level, by student, by year.

Support exporting a method as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a method from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a method to a STAG annotation if the method involves a scale-transition claim.

Support linking a method to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the method is described in a deposited work.

Support the method map: a visualisation of the methods by subject and by prerequisite.

Support the proficiency curve: a visualisation of how a student's proficiency in a method changes over time.

Support the method comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more methods for the same problem.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical method in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 methods.

Versioned: the tracker records the STAG schema version, the method schema version, and the tracker version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

